    <p class="small text-secondary">{{ c.price }}. {{ c.rating }}. <a href="/en/respira-vs-{{ c.slug }}">Full comparison with Respira</a></p>
    {%- endfor %}

    <h3>Respira</h3>
    <p>A pollen and air-quality app covering eight countries. In the United States it reports the eleven species published by the Google Pollen API, including ragweed and oak, with air quality on the EPA scale; in Europe it uses the Copernicus CAMS model, enriched with national monitoring networks in Spain, Italy and Germany. It keeps a symptom, severity and medication diary you can export as a PDF, and shows levels on Home and Lock Screen widgets.</p>
    <p class="small text-secondary">{{ R.price }}. {{ R.rating }}.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section flow" aria-labelledby="honest">
    <h2 id="honest">Where Respira is not the right answer</h2>
    <p>If you want an app to tell you how bad your own symptoms are likely to be tomorrow, Respira does not do that: it reports measured and forecast pollen, and leaves the interpretation to you. AllergyCast is built around exactly that prediction.</p>
    <p>If air quality rather than pollen is your reason for installing anything, AirVisual's coverage and history are far deeper than what Respira shows.</p>
    <p>And if a large, settled US user base is what makes you trust an app, Respira does not have one yet. It launched in the United States in August 2026 and has no US ratings so far.</p>
  </section>

